---
import { SITE } from '@/config';
import Page from '@/layouts/Page.astro';
---
<Page
  title={`About ${SITE.name}`}
  description={`Why ${SITE.name} exists, how it is funded, and what it will and will not tell you.`}
  path="/about"
  lede="An independent guide to Indian banking problems, written for the person stuck on the phone with their bank."
>
  <h2>What this site is</h2>
  <p>
    Most pages you find when a bank transaction goes wrong are written by someone who wants to sell
    you something: a bank explaining its own process, a fintech collecting a lead, an aggregator
    chasing ad impressions. They tend to describe how things are meant to work, not what to do when
    they do not.
  </p>
  <p>
    {SITE.name} is written the other way round. Every page starts from a specific thing that has
    gone wrong, states the rule that applies, and says what your actual options are — including when
    the honest answer is that you have none and should escalate.
  </p>

  <h2>Where the information comes from</h2>
  <p>
    Rules come from primary sources: RBI circulars and master directions, NPCI procedural guidelines,
    the Reserve Bank — Integrated Ombudsman Scheme, and the credit bureaus' own published policies.
    Every page lists what it relied on, with dates, at the bottom. If we cannot cite it, we do not
    claim it.
  </p>
  <p>
    Where a rule is genuinely ambiguous or banks interpret it inconsistently, we say so rather than
    picking the tidier answer.
  </p>

  <h2>How this is funded</h2>
  <p>
    Advertising, and affiliate commissions on some financial products. Pages carrying affiliate
    links say so at the top, before you read anything. Advertisers and affiliate partners get no
    say in what we write, and no page is written because a product pays well. Read the
    <a href="/affiliate-disclosure">affiliate disclosure</a> and the
    <a href="/editorial-policy">editorial policy</a> for the detail.
  </p>

  <h2>What this site is not</h2>
  <p>
    It is not financial, legal or tax advice, and it is not a substitute for talking to your bank or
    a qualified professional about your specific situation. We are not affiliated with the Reserve
    Bank of India, with any bank, or with any credit bureau. See the
    <a href="/disclaimer">full disclaimer</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>Corrections</h2>
  <p>
    If something here is wrong or out of date, tell us at <a href="/contact">the contact page</a>.
    Corrections to substantive errors are made at the top of the page in question, not quietly.
  </p>
</Page>
